Top-down architectural floorplan of a Mediterranean noble house in decline. D&D battle map style, grid squares visible (5ft/square). Two floors shown side by side.

GROUND FLOOR (left):
- ENTRANCE HALL: stone floor, coat rack, faded tapestry on wall, iron candelabra. Double doors to garden (south). Single door to corridor (north).
- CORRIDOR: narrow, connects all ground floor rooms. Worn carpet runner. Paintings on walls (landscapes - nothing suspicious).
- SALA DA PRANZO (dining room, largest room): rectangular table set for 5, wooden chairs with carved backs. Large fireplace (west wall, lit). Sideboard with wine bottles and ceramic plates. Two tall windows (south, overlooking garden). Chandelier (iron, half the candles missing). Faded wallpaper with floral pattern. Door to kitchen (east), door to corridor (west).
- KITCHEN: wood-burning stove, copper pots hanging, herbs drying from ceiling. Back door to servants' alley (east). Simple and functional - the one room that's well-maintained. Wooden staircase going up to upper floor (right side of the room, clearly visible).
- STUDY/OFFICE: desk with papers, inkwell, quill. Bookshelf (half-empty - books sold). Locked drawer (DC 18 - contains correspondence with coded initials). Window overlooking the street (north). Armchair by small fireplace.
- SITTING ROOM: two worn armchairs, small table, cold fireplace. Dusty. Clearly unused - the Marchese lives alone and doesn't entertain often.

UPPER FLOOR (right):
- LANDING: wooden staircase from ground floor. Creaky floorboards.
- MASTER BEDROOM: large bed (canopy, fabric faded), wardrobe (contains disguise materials if searched - DC 20, hidden compartment behind false back), vanity mirror, washbasin.
- GUEST ROOM: simple bed, empty wardrobe. Dusty - unused for months.
- STORAGE ROOM: locked (DC 15). Crates with old merchandise labels scratched off. Empty potion bottles in a box (Investigation DC 17 to notice they match "La Rocca Imponente" shape). Old ledger with coded entries.

EXTERIOR:
- GARDEN (south): overgrown, fountain (dry, cracked basin), two cypress trees, stone path to iron gate. Low wall with view of the port below.
- SERVANTS' ALLEY (east): narrow passage between houses, leads to back streets.

STYLE: Warm parchment tones, clean lines, labeled rooms in elegant italic font. Furniture shown as simple icons. Subtle signs of decay: cracks in walls, stains on floors, missing tiles. Mediterranean architecture - terracotta roof tiles visible in cross-section, arched doorways, thick stone walls.

COLOR PALETTE: Warm beige/cream for stone, dark brown for wood, muted gold for decorative elements, grey for areas in disrepair.

SIZE: Approximately 40x30 feet per floor. Total image: landscape orientation showing both floors side by side with labels.
